    {
      "id": "surface-meaning",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -1400,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Meaning\n\n**What is this thing?**\n\nA Surface is a named, independently-resolved presentation track. It lets one node or graph offer more than one complete way of being shown — each scoped to a different hosting context — instead of a single universal ladder every host is expected to interpret the same way.\n\nThe motivating case: a Godzilla node is perfectly ordinary on the graph canvas — glyph, icon, summary, detail, the conventional ladder. But the same node placed on a map needs a completely different presentation: a map doesn't want a \"detail card,\" it wants something that reads as a map marker at every zoom level a map actually has. Those aren't two points on one continuum. They're two different continuums, because the thing being measured — screen occupancy in a card layout vs. apparent size on a map — is different. A Surface is the name for one of those continuums. A node with map ambitions declares a second Surface; a node without them just has the one every graph already has by default.\n\nA Surface does not render anything itself. It names and scopes a **rendering ladder** — an ordered set of Views, each claiming its own point on that Surface's resolution scale — that a host resolves against."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-invariants",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Invariants",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -860,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Invariants\n\n**What makes this thing a Surface, specifically?**\n\n- Every graph has at least one Surface, the default one — every graph already has this today, whether or not anyone thinks of it as \"a Surface.\"\n- A Surface is identified by an `id`, unique within its Declaration's `surfaces[]`.\n- A Surface owns a **rendering ladder**: an ordered, open-ended chain of Views. \"Open-ended\" is not aspirational — `viewResolution.js`'s `authoredViewResolution()` already lets a View claim *any* continuous resolution value, not one of a fixed set of named bands (`view.data.semanticZoom.resolution`, any number below `FULL_GRAPH_RESOLUTION`). A ladder can have three rungs or thirty.\n- A View declares which Surface's ladder it belongs to — the Surface doesn't enumerate its Views; the Views name their Surface. This mirrors an already-real convention: `normalizeSurfacePort()` in `graphNodeContract.js` already reads `data.surfaceId` off Port nodes today, for a different purpose. Extending that same field to Views keeps one convention instead of inventing a second.\n- A host resolves a graph by first selecting the Surface that matches its own context, then resolving richest-fitting *within that Surface's own ladder* — never mixing rungs from two Surfaces into one decision.\n- A host that finds no Surface authored for its context falls back to the default Surface. A graph that never authors a second Surface behaves exactly as every graph does today — this primitive adds a capability, it doesn't obsolete the common case."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-contract",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Contract",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -320,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Contract\n\n**What data belongs to it?**\n\nToday, real, on the Declaration:\n\n```text\ndeclaration.surfaces[] { id, kind, label, portNodeId, viewNodeId, exposes }\n```\n\nProposed by this primitive — not yet implemented:\n\n```text\ndeclaration.surfaces[].context   // what this Surface's ladder is FOR: 'graph' (default,\n                                  // implicit), 'map', or whatever a future host names itself\nview.data.surfaceId              // which Surface's ladder this View is a rung of\nview.data.semanticZoom.resolution  // this View's point on that ladder — already real,\n                                  // already continuous (authoredViewResolution in\n                                  // viewResolution.js); the new part is scoping it per-Surface\n                                  // instead of one shared graph-wide ladder\n```\n\n`context` is the one genuinely new field. Everything else this primitive needs — continuous resolution values, a chain of Views, richest-fitting selection — already exists; it just isn't scoped per-Surface yet. Today there is exactly one implicit ladder per graph, anchored at the Declaration's `default-view`/`summary-view`/`icon-view` relationships and `view-sequence` edges (see the Declaration primitive's Ports and Relationships section). This primitive's job is to let a graph own more than one."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-ports",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Ports and Relationships",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 220,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Ports and Relationships\n\n**What semantic relationships does it expose?**\n\nNone directly — Surface is not a node type. There is no `type:'surface'` node to drop on a canvas. It's expressed structurally, in two places:\n\n- An entry in the Declaration's `surfaces[]` array, which names the Surface and (proposed) states what context it's for.\n- Each participating View's own `data.surfaceId`, declaring which Surface's ladder it's a rung of, plus its own `semanticZoom.resolution` stating where on that ladder it sits.\n\nThe Declaration's existing `default-view`/`summary-view`/`icon-view`/`view-sequence` relationships (see the Declaration primitive) remain exactly what they are today — they're the anchors and chain for the **default** Surface specifically, the one every graph already has. A second Surface doesn't get its own new relationship vocabulary on the Declaration; it gets its own `surfaces[]` entry and its Views tag themselves into it directly."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-behavior",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Behavior",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 760,
        "y": -560
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Behavior\n\n**What does it participate in — and what's real today versus proposed?**\n\n**Real today, for the one implicit default Surface every graph has:**\n- `presentWorkspaceInstances()` in `graphWorkspace.js` already resolves a continuous workspace occupancy value and, when a graph has authored numeric-resolution Views (`hasNumericViews`), calls `resolvePercentageGraphView()` to pick among them via `selectViewAtResolution()` — richest View whose threshold is still met. This is a working, live, open-ended (not 3-or-4-band) resolution mechanism. It just isn't described as \"a Surface\" anywhere in the code, because there's only ever been the one.\n- `representationResolver.js`'s `resolveRichestRepresentation()` is a second, more general resolver (space-based rather than a single resolution scalar) built the same session as this primitive, also open-ended by design, not yet wired into any live selection path.\n\n**Proposed, not yet built:**\n- Scoping a rendering ladder to a named Surface, so a graph can own more than one: e.g. a `default` Surface's ladder for the ordinary graph canvas, and a `map` Surface's ladder for a map host — authored independently, each with its own Views at its own resolution points.\n- A host declaring which Surface it wants. A map viewport would ask a graph for its `context: 'map'` Surface specifically, falling back to `default` when the graph never authored one. Nothing today lets a host state its own context or a Surface state what context it's for — this is the one real gap, not the resolution math.\n- `collectViewSequence()`/`resolveViewByName()` in `viewSequence.js` currently walk one graph-wide chain rooted at the Declaration. Making them Surface-aware means filtering by `data.surfaceId` before walking, not a different walking algorithm."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-representations",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Representations",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 1300,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Representations\n\n**How is it represented?**\n\nNever directly. A Surface itself has no visual form — what renders is always whichever View its rendering ladder resolves to, for the host and resolution asking. Two different hosts looking at the same node \"at the same moment\" may be showing Views from two entirely different Surfaces, each independently correct for its own context — a map viewport and the graph canvas are not disagreeing with each other; they were never asking the same question."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-boundary",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -1400,
        "y": 260
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 360,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive\n\n- Surface does not replace View, Content, or Glyph. It groups and scopes them; it doesn't render in their place.\n- Surface is not a Bridge, and a Surface's rendering ladder is not the same thing as a Bridge's `role:'export'` \"kit\" exposure (see the Bridge primitive). One is about which *classes or resources* a graph offers to an importer; this one is about which *Views* a graph offers to a *hosting context*. Worth being honest that today's `declaration.surfaces[]` already conflates both — a surface entry can be `kind:'export'` (Bridge-exposure) or `kind:'port'/'view'` (presentation) in the same array, with nothing distinguishing the two purposes structurally yet. This primitive doesn't resolve that overlap, only names it.\n- This primitive does not specify negotiation beyond exact-context-match-or-default-fallback. A host asking for a context no graph has ever heard of, or a \"closest available\" match between related contexts, is out of scope for now.\n- Surface does not define what a map (or any other non-graph-canvas host) actually *does* with what it receives — only that it can ask for and receive a ladder scoped to it. The map renderer itself is not part of this primitive."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "surface-examples",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Examples",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -860,
        "y": 260
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 360,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "cdff8406-c80e-455f-be75-2cca43aa841f"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Examples\n\n**Examples live outside the primitive, referenced, not embedded.**\n\nNone yet. No graph has authored a second, non-default Surface — this primitive was written from the design conversation that motivated it (the Godzilla-on-a-map case), before any graph needed one for real. Add a portal here once a real graph exercises a second Surface, the same discipline followed for the Declaration and Bridge primitives' own Examples sections."
        }
      }
    }
